    <exercise>
        <title>Wing-Flapping Frequency of Birds</title>
        
        <p>
            A hovering animal (like a hummingbird) must use its wings to generate a lift force that is equal in 
            magnitude to its weight. It does this by flapping its wings and pushing air downwards. This process can be 
            complicated, but our goal is to obtain a simple estimate of how often a bird must flap its wings: 
            this will be a frequency f (beats per second)
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find an approximate formula for what this lift force should depend on. Clearly define any 
                    parameters you introduce, such as the length <m>L</m> of the wings. At this point, its fine to 
                    keep your lift force in terms of a speed instead of trying to introduce the frequency.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Now figure out how to relate, approximately, the speed you used in the previous part to the 
                    wing beating frequency <m>f</m> (hint: frequency has units of 1/time)
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    	Let us now make the gross estimate that all animals have roughly the same density <m>\rho</m>. 
                        Using your assumption, find how the frequency f scales with animal size: that is, you should find 
                        an expression of the type <m>~{f=CL^n}</m>. The constant <m>C</m> isn't particularly important 
                        (it can depend on lots of things that we aren't taking into account here!), but the power n can be 
                        compared to experiment. This is an example of an <term>allometric scaling relation</term>
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>